implicit_bangla:
সে খুব বিস্মিত ছিল;  
তার চোখেমুখে বিস্ময়ের ছাপ স্পষ্ট ছিল;  
সে যা দেখছিল, তা তার কাছে নতুন বা অপ্রত্যাশিত ছিল।

implicit_english:
He was deeply astonished;  
the look of surprise was clear on his face;  
what he was seeing was new or unexpected to him.

implicit_russian:
Он был очень удивлён;  
на его лице ясно читалось изумление;  
то, что он видел, было для него новым или неожиданным.

text_english:
Here is an accurate and natural translation, preserving the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

At the end of life's great journey, may I find refuge in the shelter of your loving embrace;  
May the joys of two hearts, the sorrows of two hearts, and the hopes of two hearts all merge at your feet.

Here is the translation, preserving the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

Give me anew, let me be renewed with new adornments.  
In the curse of Hemanta, the woodland lies barren and destitute;

Come in this awakening, vibrant dawn;  
Come to the city, the fields, the forests;  
Come in our work, our words, our hearts.  
Come with anklets ringing at your feet;  
Come in the melodious voices filled with song.

text_russian:
Ты сделал меня бесконечным — такова твоя игра: опустошив, ты снова наполнил меня новой и новой жизнью.  
Сколько гор, сколько берегов рек я обошёл, неся эту маленькую флейту;  
сколько мелодий ты вновь и вновь играл через неё — кому я смогу это рассказать?

text_english:
Here is the English translation, preserving the nuances and metaphors:

Your body—it was yours, and you brought it here once;  
Then, the crowd of people, night and day, called you away—where, I never knew—  
Now, these eyes have grown weary;  
I have been torn, I have been ripped apart,  
Walking the roads of this world…

Here is an accurate and natural translation that preserves the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

Even after all this, there is still silence;  
Even after all this, a time of restraint;  
Even after all this, flags fluttering in the wind;  
Even after all this, a steadfast convoy;  
Even after all this, doubts of the past;  
Even after all this, gleaming salutes;  
Even after all this, the only goal is victory;  
Even after all this, the forces remain ready.

Here is the English translation, preserving the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

Sorrow is the flower of my garden, sorrow is the voice of my heart;  
Without sorrow, my life would have been nothing but empty.  
Sorrow is the rhythm of my poetry, in sorrow I am captive;  
It is sorrow that has become my companion through a thousand nights.  
I am a connoisseur of sorrow, and so I dearly love my sorrow.

Here is the English translation, preserving the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

Sorrow is the adornment of my mind, sorrow is my mind’s pride;  
Sorrow has swept away all the joy from my heart today.  
Sorrow is the language of my eyes, sorrow is my endless weeping;  
Sorrow has stolen from me my moon-like smile.  
I am a connoisseur of sorrow, and that is why I dearly love my sorrow.

Here is the English translation, preserving metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

Sorrow is my southern breeze, sorrow is the rain’s water;  
Sorrow has taught me how to firmly grasp the helm today.  
Sorrow is my night of the new moon, sorrow is my sudden hailstorm;  
Sorrow has given me the vision of three mountains today.  
I am a connoisseur of sorrow, and so I dearly love my sorrow.

Here is the English translation, preserving the nuances, metaphors, and cultural context:

If I get just two handfuls of food twice a day, I’ll let go of all other demands;  
I have no unreasonable greed, not even any carnal hunger—  
I don’t desire the sari draped below the navel, nor its owner;  
Whoever wants them can take them—give them to anyone you wish;  
Know this: I have no need for any of that.

Here is a faithful and natural translation that preserves the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

I do not accept interrogation under a thousand-watt light glaring into my eyes day and night;  
I do not accept needles under my nails, being laid out on blocks of ice;  
I do not accept being hung by my feet until blood drips from my nose;  
I do not accept boots on my lips, wounds all over my body from burning rods;  
I do not accept sharp whips tearing my back to pieces, suddenly doused with alcohol.

text_russian:
Вот перевод на русский язык, с сохранением метафор, эмоций и скрытого смысла:

Загремел барабанщик Дхьяра Габу, получив приказ от царя:  
«Больше никто не смеет плакать — ни мужчина, ни женщина;  
Что бы ни случилось, как бы ни было горько, — всё равно придётся смеяться,  
Таков приказ великого царя, что станет владыкой;  
А кто ослушается царского повеления — взойдёт на кол!»

text_english:
Here is an accurate and natural translation that preserves the metaphors, emotions, and cultural context:

Joy, regret, anger—at whose command flowers bloom in the wilderness by mere will;  
Who brings the enchanting fragrance of parijat blossoms from the heavenly gardens;  
In the desert—satisfied in contemplation of whom, gentle rivers flow murmuring like water!
